t will likely be  somewhere in between  there is no one right way to respond to  these changes so we should expect  different responses  every person's experience through the  pandemic has been unique depending on  the challenges they have faced  coveted risk to individuals and the  population is not a binary on off or yes  no state  some people will continue to be at high  risk individually of having a severe  outcome should they be infected  as a population we will continue in the  short term to be impacted by the coveted  patient load on hospitals which is being  managed capably by healthcare teams but  is not yet back to a baseline state  also both as individuals and together as  a population we have all had impacts in  our lives from the protective measures  that have been necessary to use  throughout our various ways  as we move forward it will be vital that  we find a way to respect each other's  previous experiences comfort levels and  risk context  different as they may be from our own  this includes seeking to understand and  provide space for actions of others  especially if they are taking different  precautions than the ones we are taking  i particularly want to thank parents and  educators for their work to support our  kids to make a transition to a masking  optional school approach that started  yesterday  this change is hard  and our kids need us to support them and  to model how to respect others despite  differences  masking remains a very prudent public  health measure and those who are wearing  masks need to be supported  those who are no longer wearing masks  also need support so this single issue  does not further drive wedges in our  communities  in general i ask that all of us strive  to be mindful of the fact that we don't  know what factors are driving the  decisions of those around us  if you're not sure what someone is  comfortable with please don't assume  instead ask them and respect their  choices  this can apply to everything from  considering whether to plan gatherings  indoors or outdoors  asking about masking preferences when in  other people's homes  and planning for the upcoming return to  the workplace in ways that respect and  consider different levels of risk in  those who are impacted by change  one thing covit has shown us time and  time again  is how connected we are all to each  other and how our actions impact those  around us  as we move through this time of  transition  i ask that all of us stay mindful of  that fact and that we think of others as  well as ourselves as we go about our  daily lives  over the last few days i have heard some  reports of people refusing to wear masks  in public settings  i want to remind albertans that until we  are in step two masking is required in  indoor places across the province  masking is especially important in  health care settings  health care is different from other  public places and ahs and other health  care providers will continue to have  additional measures and precautions in  acute care and continuing care  facilities to help protect vulnerable  patients and residents  these measures are also keeping health  care workers safe  i ask all albertans who may need to  access any health care facility in the  coming weeks to be respectful of the  rules in place even if they're different  from other settings  by doing so you are helping to keep  patients residents and staff in these  facilities safe  finally i want to let you know about  plans for media availabilities over the  next few weeks  my team has tried over the last two  years to support the importance of  personal well-being and self-care for  those working on the response  as part of this we have been working  recently to set up a rotation of time  away from work and it is now my  opportunity to have some time off  after this week i will be away for two  weeks  minister copping will provide regular  covet updates to media during that time  and my team will continue to monitor the  data and provide input on the response  there will also not be a covered media  availability this thursday  thank you and we're happy to take  questions thanks dr hinshaw and minister  no one in the room here so we'll go  straight to the phone first caller  please  thank you matt woodman ctv  hi there my question is for the health  minister um when you and the premier  outlined the public health measure  changes last week the premier said that  changes were based on trends in other  countries where omicron had emerged  before it had in alberta and had peaked  before it did in alberta the uk for  example we're now seeing many schools in  the uk are bringing mass back for kids  are you worried this is something that's  going to have to happen here as well  even though it was just dropped  yesterday  yeah not at this time what we're seeing  generally in a number of those countries  uk  and also we see in trends in ireland  that  the numbers are still trending down in  terms of both hospitalizations uh and  actually in terms of cases trending down  or maybe plateaued a little bit but  we're going to continue to watch it very  closely and and as i said before if we  need to make adjustments we will but  we're going to watch our own data and we  haven't seen  um a spike in hospitalizations in those  countries  thanks minister i'm matt follow-up  uh that's it for me today thank you  there's a first uh thanks matt so we'll  go to the next caller please  thank you jennifer lee cbc  hi thanks for taking uh my question and  this kind of dovetails off uh the  question you just had  you know there is a lot of concern about  the decision to lift  masking in schools  a number of physicians pediatricians  infectious disease folks  you know they're worried about the  implications of this decision  with only roughly about 20 of kids in  that five to 11 year old age range with  two doses so how do you respond to their  concerns that this is too early that it  could lead to increased transmission  followed by increased hospitalizations  and and how do you respond to their  calls to reinstate that math mandate or  requirement in school  yeah as we indicated last week when we  made the announcement you know we did an  assessment on a variety of uh of  measures recognizing that  the trends were heading in the right  direction hospitalizations as we as  shown earlier today uh new  hospitalizations have been dropping  steadily  and so we took a look at what are all  the measures in place uh look at the  measures that that you know may be less  effective or potential for for causing  harm so the rep uh we remove that as as  sort of phase one but also in regards to  masking and masking for for children  there's you know mixed evidence on the  uh on the efficacy and you know concerns  being raised about you know impacting  child learning  so  this was our first stage  we  put a program in place in terms of  reducing restrictions at a very stage  approach so we can actually watch what  happens over the uh the next couple of  weeks i understand some of the concerns  uh you know raised by uh by parents  there's different degrees of of comfort  levels as indicated by dr  dr henshaw we are in a transition phase  uh some people are  you know willing and eager to uh  to move forward and others are are  concerned we need to be respectful of  individual choices but now's the time to  make the transition move to individual  choices by uh by parents and uh and  their children and then we'll watch the  data  thanks minister uh jen follow up  yeah i do thanks i i think one of the  concerns you know  that we're hearing is that  first of all the the  the mask requirement is being lifted but  also that it's not being done in tandem  with other  uh measures that might sort of  compensate for that for example um  trying to address that low vaccination  rate among kids through things like  making vaccines more accessible in  different places so you know some of the  things i've heard is suggestions about  um you know making them available in  schools which i i know you've done with  the older kids  even after hours for example we're  hearing calls about  allowing family doctors offices to  provide them more pharmacies and even  places where kids go often places like  hockey rinks things like that so  what is the uh your government's  intention in terms of trying to make  this vaccine more accessible and if you  could comment on sort of each of those  types of locations that we're hearing  calls for the province to make them  available if you don't mind  no no thanks for the question no there  are some measures that remain in schools  as you know  cohorting for example uh those remaining  schools in regards to vaccinations  government we agree that vaccinations  are the the  uh the best  way to protect all of us and it's not  only about coming out of omicron  although that's that's you know the  numbers are coming down and we are on  the the other side of the fifth wave but  it's also protecting against future  waves and quite frankly we have to  normalize vaccines um to be able to like  covet vaccines just like everyone gets  the annual flu vaccine we may very well  need to be moving to that um now whether  it's an annual or every six months we'll  have to wait and see  you know my focus and we appointed a uh  an advisory committee on vaccination uh  to be able to provide uh advice to our  government and to to myself about how do  we improve uh vaccination rates at kids  it's just uh roughly uh 46 percent  um  for uh five to 11 year olds uh at this  point in time we need to get that higher  so we're looking at other options in  terms of doing that but when we talked  originally about  vaccines at school  when we did that for older older kids it  wasn't very successful in terms of the  rate but but again we'll we'll take a  look at our the various different  approaches over the coming weeks to be  able to support vaccine vaccination  because this is going to be with us for  a long time just like people who get flu  vaccines will likely need to get coveted  vaccines  thanks minister uh turns out we only had  two reporters waiting on the phone and  no one else so that's it we'll wrap it  up there uh thanks minister and thanks  dr hinshaw thank you
 

